= The New Departure

A faction of <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Northern Democrats**</span>, tired of losing on the same issues every election, is trying something new. Led by men like August Belmont and promoted by the New York World, they want the party to accept Reconstruction as settled law and stop campaigning on its reversal.

Their argument is simple: the war is over, the reforms have been enacted and opposing them has cost the <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrat**</span> election after election. They would rather concede the past and challenge the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> on issues where they are more vulnerable.

The idea has its opponents. Old-guard <span style="color: #002765;">**Bourbon Democrats**</span>, particularly in the South, see it as surrender. The "New Departure" faction is nevertheless gaining ground at state conventions across the North. Reform-minded <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span>, already disillusioned with their own party, are watching closely.

The <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> are wary. A <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic Party**</span> that stops promising to undo Reconstruction could make it easier for dissatisfied <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> to defect.